["Here's an example of how you could structure a Java application to meet these requirements. Note that this is only a basic outline and does not include actual implementation details. 

Please note that actual implementation will require much more complex code than can be provided here due to the specific nature of each requirement, including integration with external APIs, error handling, thread safety, logging & monitoring, rate limiting, and extensibility.

```java
public class PokemonService {
    private ExternalAPIClient apiClient;
    private LoadBalancer loadBalancer;
    private Cache cache;
    private RateLimiter rateLimiter;

    public PokemonService(ExternalAPIClient apiClient, LoadBalancer loadBalancer, Cache cache, RateLimiter rateLimiter) {
        this.apiClient = apiClient;
        this.loadBalancer = loadBalancer;
        this.cache = cache;
        this.rateLimiter = rateLimiter;
    }

    public PokemonDetails getPokemonDetails(String pokemonName) {
        if (!rateLimiter.allowRequest()) {
            throw new RateLimitExceededException();
        }

        synchronized (this) {
            PokemonDetails details = cache.get(pokemonName);

            if (details == null) {
                String serverAddress = loadBalancer.nextServer();
                details = apiClient.fetchPokemonData(serverAddress, pokemonName);

                cache.put(pokemonName, details);
            }

            return details;
        }
    }
}
```
This service makes use of several classes that encapsulate the complexity of different aspects of the system:

- `ExternalAPIClient` is responsible for fetching Pokemon data from an external API. It should handle potential API failures and network disruptions.

- `LoadBalancer` distributes incoming requests across multiple servers to ensure high availability, it also handles server failure and retries request on another server if one fails.

- `Cache` stores the results of API calls for efficiency. This class should also handle cache eviction policies and provide a fallback mechanism when API calls fail.

- `RateLimiter` controls how often requests can be made to the external API, to prevent abuse or overload. It should dynamically adjust limits based on user behavior and current load.

Each of these classes could also have their own set of tests to ensure they are working as expected in isolation. 

This is a simplified representation of how you might structure such a service. In reality, the implementation would be much more complex, with additional layers for configuration, error handling, logging, and monitoring. Also, it's recommended to use proven third-party libraries and frameworks where possible to reduce code complexity and potential issues.
