["Implementing such complex functionality in C requires deep knowledge of C concepts, including low-level memory management (to implement a semaphore), threading primitives, and an understanding of the external logging framework's API and constraints. 

Here is a simplified example based on your requirements. In real world scenarios, these would be modularized into separate files and possibly reusable libraries for better maintainability:

```C
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

// Define a Semaphore struct that holds its name, semaphore instance, 
// initial and maximum values, and other necessary information.
typedef struct {
    char *name;
    sem_t semaphore;
    int maxCount;
} Semaphore;

Semaphore* createSemaphore(const char *name, int initVal, int maxLimit) {
    if (initVal > maxLimit || initVal < 0) {
        return NULL; // Invalid values, log an error or throw exception.
    }

    Semaphore *sem = malloc(sizeof(Semaphore));
    sem->name = strdup(name);
    sem->maxCount = maxLimit;

    if (sem_init(&sem->semaphore, 0, initVal) != 0) {
        return NULL; // Semaphore initialization failed.
    }

    return sem;
}

void deleteSemaphore(Semaphore *sem) {
    if (sem == NULL) {
        return;
    }

    sem_destroy(&sem->semaphore);
    free(sem->name);
    free(sem);
}

// Wrap the necessary P/V operations of a semaphore in functions. 
void semaphoreWait(Semaphore *sem) {
    // Implement concurrency safety checks and logging here, e.g., check if the semaphore's value is already at max, etc.
    sem_wait(&sem->semaphore);
}

void semaphorePost(Semaphore *sem) {
    // Here you can also implement concurrency safety checks and logging.
    sem_post(&sem->semaphore);
}
```

This code implements the key functionality of creating, using, deleting, and managing a semaphore. However, it does not include detailed error handling, metrics reporting or integration with an external logging framework. 

These would all be additional complex features that would need to be implemented in separate modules/classes in a full-featured system. For instance, metrics reporting could be achieved by adding counters for the semaphore's current and maximum values and updating them accordingly whenever `sem_post()` or `sem_wait()` is called. 

As C does not have built-in support for concurrent data structures like maps (which would be useful to map semaphore names to their instances), manual locking might be required when accessing these collections in a multithreaded environment. Logging could be implemented by calling the external logging framework's API functions from within these helper functions, while ensuring that this does not impact performance too much. 

Please note that designing such a system is complex and would likely involve a lot of additional code, including but not limited to error handling, metrics tracking, concurrency safety checks, etc. A real-world solution might use existing libraries or tools to simplify these tasks. For example, the [Concord logging framework](https://github.com/CodaProtocol/concord) could be integrated for logging, and an external monitoring service like Prometheus could be used for metrics reporting.
