['Here is a simplified example of how you might start implementing this system in Java. This code does not cover all required engineering requirements such as distributed processing or error handling but it provides a good starting point for designing your service. Please note that creating an actual, production-ready solution requires careful consideration and rigorous testing to ensure correctness and stability.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class DisjointPairs {
    private int[] parent;  // parent[i] = parent of i
    private byte[] rank;   // rank[i] = rank of subtree rooted at i (never more than 31)
    private int count;     // number of components

    public DisjointPairs(int n) {
        if (n < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
        count = n;
        parent = new int[n];
        rank = new byte[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            parent[i] = i;
            rank[i] = 0;
        }
    }

    public int find(int p) {
        validate(p);
        while (p != parent[p]) {
            parent[p] = parent[parent[p]];    // path compression by halving
            p = parent[p];
        }
        return p;
    }

    public int count() {
        return count;
    }

    public boolean connected(int p, int q) {
        return find(p) == find(q);
    }

    public void union(int p, int q) {
        int rootP = find(p);
        int rootQ = find(q);
        if (rootP == rootQ) return;

        // make root of smaller rank point to root of larger rank
        if      (rank[rootP] < rank[rootQ]) parent[rootP] = rootQ;
        else if (rank[rootP] > rank[rootQ]) parent[rootQ] = rootP;
        else {
            parent[rootQ] = rootP;
            rank[rootP]++;
        }
        count--;
    }

    private void validate(int p) {
        int n = parent.length;
        if (p < 0 || p >= n) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("index " + p + " is not between 0 and " + (n-1));
        }
    }
}
```
This class represents a union–find data type with integer values from `0` to `N - 1`. The constructor creates an initial set of elements, each in their own component. The `union` method merges the components containing the given elements. The `connected` method checks whether two elements are in the same component and finally, the `count` method returns the number of remaining components.
']

