['Here is the code for UserManagement class with updateUserPassword method along with other helper classes. This doesn't cover all aspects mentioned due to character limit restrictions but gives you an idea on how to structure your solution.

```php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);

use \Exception;
use Psr\Log\LoggerInterface;

class InvalidPasswordException extends Exception { }

interface AuthService 
{
    public function verifyPasswordStrength(string $password): bool;
}

interface DatabaseConnection 
{
    public function executeQuery($query, array $params);
}

class UserManagement 
{
    private $authService;
    private $dbConnection;
    private $logger;

    public function __construct(AuthService $authService, DatabaseConnection $dbConnection, LoggerInterface $logger) {
        $this->authService = $authService;
        $this->dbConnection = $dbConnection;
        $this->logger = $logger;
    }

    public function updateUserPassword(string $userId, string $newPassword): void 
    {
        try {
            if (!$this->authService->verifyPasswordStrength($newPassword)) {
                throw new InvalidPasswordException('The password is not strong enough');
            }

            $query = "UPDATE users SET password=:password WHERE id=:id";
            $params = [':id' => $userId, ':password' => password_hash($newPassword, PASSWORD_DEFAULT)];

            $this->dbConnection->beginTransaction();
            $this->dbConnection->executeQuery($query, $params);
            $this->dbConnection->commit();
        } catch (InvalidPasswordException $e) {
            $this->logger->error('Failed to update password', [
                'userId' => $userId, 
                'message' => $e->getMessage()
            ]);
            throw $e; // rethrow the exception
        } catch (Throwable $t) {
            $this->dbConnection->rollback();

            $this->logger->error('Failed to update password', [
                'userId' => $userId, 
                'message' => $t->getMessage(),
                'trace' => $t->getTraceAsString()
            ]);
        }
    }
}
```

To improve performance, you can consider adding cache for the authentication service responses and also optimizing database queries by indexing fields that are used in WHERE clause of your SQL queries.

