['I can provide a high-level overview of how you might design this caching system in C++. But note that full implementation requires a lot more work than can be covered here due to its complexity and size, especially the integration with external systems mentioned in the task description. 

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <list>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <mutex>
#include <chrono>

class CacheEntry {
public:
    std::string key;
    std::string value;
    std::chrono::time_point<std::chrono::system_clock> timestamp;
    // Add more relevant fields as necessary
};

class LRUCache {
private:
    int capacity, timeToLive;
    std::list<CacheEntry> itemList;
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::list<CacheEntry>::iterator> cacheMap;
    std::mutex mtx;
public:
    LRUCache(int capacity, int timeToLive) : capacity(capacity), timeToLive(timeToLive){}

    std::string get(std::string key) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        // Check if key exists in cache
        if (cacheMap.find(key) == cacheMap.end()) return "-1";

        // If expired, remove from cache
        if ((std::chrono::system_clock::now() - cacheMap[key]->timestamp).count() > timeToLive) {
            itemList.erase(cacheMap[key]);
            cacheMap.erase(key);
            return "-1";
        }

        // Move the accessed entry to front
        itemList.splice(itemList.begin(), itemList, cacheMap[key]);
        return cacheMap[key]->value;
    }

    void set(std::string key, std::string value) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        CacheEntry newItem = { key, value, std::chrono::system_clock::now() };

        // If cache is full, remove least recently used item
        if (itemList.size() == capacity) {
            cacheMap.erase(itemList.back().key);
            itemList.pop_back();
        }

        // Add new entry to front of list and update map
        itemList.push_front(newItem);
        cacheMap[key] = itemList.begin();
    }
};
``` 
This is a basic implementation of an LRU Cache in C++ without any error handling or additional functionalities like POST, monitoring service integration etc. A production-grade solution would require much more complex code and testing. Additionally, this only covers caching headers, but you'd also need to consider how to handle concurrent access by multiple threads, secure data handling, backpressure handling mechanisms, integrating with external systems, performance metrics collection and analytics, etc. 

This would be a good starting point for further development based on your requirements. As mentioned before, this is only a simplified overview of a complex system - a full implementation requires much more work and careful planning.
