['Sure, I will provide you with a basic implementation of the requested function `insertAfterFirstOccurrence` in C++. This version assumes that we have access to appropriate synchronization primitives like mutexes and condition variables for thread safety and uses a doubly-linked forward list because it supports efficient insertion operations after a node, unlike the singly linked forward_list class in standard library.

```cpp
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <mutex>
#include <iterator>

class SynchronizedForwardList {
    std::forward_list<std::string> list;
    std::mutex mtx; // mutex for thread safety
public:
    void insertAfterFirstOccurrence(const std::string& target, const std::string& str) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        auto prev = list.before_begin();
        auto it = list.begin();
        bool found = false;
        while (it != list.end()) {
            if (*it == target) {
                found = true;
                break;
            }
            ++prev;
            ++it;
        }

        if(found){
            list.insert_after(it, str);
        } else {
            list.push_back(str);
        }
    }
};
```
This code includes a `SynchronizedForwardList` class with an `insertAfterFirstOccurrence` function that ensures thread safety by locking on the mutex. The string is inserted after the first occurrence of the target string in the list, or appended to the end if the target string is not found.

Please note that due to complexity and size limitations, I cannot provide a complete system implementing all requirements. This code simply presents one possible approach and would need to be adapted and extended for a real-world application. In addition, handling malformed data or edge cases (like null pointers) is not covered in this simple example.
']

