['Here is a basic outline of how you might approach this task using Node.js with Express. This is just an example and doesn't include all the features mentioned as it would make the response too large, but it should provide a starting point for you to build on. 

Please note that you need to handle real-world scenarios like error handling and edge cases. Also consider using async/await or promises for managing asynchronous operations properly.

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const multer  = require('multer');
const {Storage} = require('@google-cloud/storage');
const vision = require('@google-cloud/vision');
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const rateLimit = require("express-rate-limit");
const axios = require('axios');

const app = express();
const upload = multer({ dest: 'uploads/' });

// configure cloud storage providers
const gcs = new Storage();
const s3 = new AWS.S3();

app.post('/upload', upload.single('image'), async (req, res) => {
  const bucketName = `bucket-${Date.now()}`;

  // Create a bucket in Google Cloud Storage
  await gcs.createBucket(bucketName);

  // Upload image to the bucket
  await gcs.bucket(bucketName).upload(req.file.path);

  // Upload image to AWS S3
  const s3Params = {
    Bucket: bucketName,
    Key: req.file.originalname,
    Body: fs.createReadStream(req.file.path),
  };
  await s3.upload(s3Params).promise();

  // Process image with Google Cloud Vision API
  const client = new vision.ImageAnnotatorClient();
  const [result] = await client.annotateImage({image: req.file});
  const labels = result.labelAnnotations;

  res.json({gcsUrl: `gs://${bucketName}/${req.file.filename}`, s3Url: `https://s3.amazonaws.com/${bucketName}/${req.file.originalname}`, labels});
});

// Rate limiting middleware 
const limiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15 minutes
  max: 100 // limit each IP to 100 requests per windowMs
});
app.use(limiter);

// Apply the rate limiting middleware to all requests
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.header("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", "*"); // update to match the domain you will make the request from
  res.header("Access-Control-Allow-Headers", "Origin, X-Requested-With, Content-Type, Accept");
  next();
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server is running on port 3000'));
```

This code creates an Express server with a single POST route `/upload` that accepts multipart form data containing an image file to be uploaded and processes it using Google Cloud Vision API before returning the processed image URLs along with labels.

To keep things simple, it doesn't include features such as image processing or caching, error handling, monitoring, database setup, integration of third-party APIs, and so on. These would require a much more detailed implementation based on your specific use case. 

Also, you need to replace bucket name with your actual GCS/S3 buckets. You should also secure your API by validating the request payload, adding authentication and authorization checks, and implementing HTTPS for better security. 

You should also consider using a task queue (like RabbitMQ or AWS SQS) to handle processing tasks asynchronously. This will ensure that even under high load, your server can keep up with incoming requests. 

For monitoring and logging, you might want to integrate with a service like Prometheus/Grafana for metric collection and visualization.
