["Creating this system requires a deep understanding of distributed systems, concurrency control mechanisms, network programming, and cloud service integration. As C++ does not natively support all these features, we would need to use additional libraries such as Boost.Asio for networking, Google Cloud Monitoring API for logging, etc.

Due to the complexity and size of this task, it's not feasible to provide a complete code solution in one go. However, I can give you an example of how to start with creating a basic Semaphore class:

```cpp
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

class Semaphore {
public:
    explicit Semaphore(size_t count = 0) : count_(count) {}

    void notify() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);
        ++count_;
        cv_.notify_one();
    }

    void wait() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);
        while (count_ == 0) {
            cv_.wait(lock);
        }
        --count_;
    }

private:
    std::mutex mtx_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
    size_t count_;
};
```
This class creates a semaphore with an initial count. The wait() method will block if the count is 0, and notify() increments the count by one and notifies any waiting threads.

However, implementing a full-featured distributed semaphore system involves considerations such as network latency, cloud service API limits, thread safety, logging infrastructure integration, metric collection and reporting, auto-scaling strategies for efficient resource usage, trade-offs between performance, maintainability, and usability, etc., which cannot be covered in one go. 

