1) PHP Service:
```php
<?php
// PDO connection to MySQL database
$pdo = new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=clinic_records', 'root', '');

function updateRecord($id, $status, $ip){
    global $pdo;
    $stmt = $pdo->prepare("START TRANSACTION");
    $stmt->execute();

    try {
        $stmt = $pdo->prepare("UPDATE records SET status = :status, last_modified_IP = :ip WHERE id = :id");
        $stmt->bindParam(':id', $id);
        $stmt->bindParam(':status', $status);
        $stmt->bindParam(':ip', $ip);

        $stmt->execute();
    } catch (Exception $e) {
        // Rollback transaction on error
        $pdo->rollBack();
        throw new Exception("Error updating record:" . $e->getMessage());
    }

    $stmt = $pdo->prepare("COMMIT");
    $stmt->execute();
}
?>
```
This is a very simplified example of how you might implement an update operation in PHP. It uses PDO for database interaction, and handles atomic operations using transactions. Error handling is implemented to ensure data integrity. For production use cases, this would need to be expanded with additional checks for input validity and error handling.

