['Here's a general outline of how you could approach this task using Node.js with libraries such as `axios` for HTTP requests and `lru-cache` for caching responses. You may need to adjust or add more based on specific requirements.

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const axios = require('axios');
const LRUCache = require("lodash/LRUCache");

class TestManager {
  constructor(config) {
    this.config = config;
    this.cache = new LRUCache({ max: 50 }); // Adjust cache size as necessary
    this.app = express();
    this.results = {};

    this.app.use(express.json());

    this.app.post('/runTests', async (req, res) => {
      const testFunctions = req.body;

      for (const fn of testFunctions) {
        try {
          // Clone configuration and apply changes based on the function's requirements
          const modifiedConfig = Object.assign({}, this.config);

          if(fn.timeout){
            setTimeout(() => {
              throw new Error("Test timed out");
            }, fn.timeout * 1000)
          }

          // Execute test function and store result in cache with its label as key
          const response = await fn(modifiedConfig);
          this.cache.set(fn.label, response.data);
          this.results[fn.label] = "Passed";
        } catch (error) {
          console.log(`Test ${fn.label} failed: ${error.message}`);
          this.results[fn.label] = `Failed - ${error.message}`;
        }
      }

      res.json(this.results);
    });

    this.app.listen(3000, () => {
      console.log('Server is listening on port 3000');
    });
  }
}

module.exports = TestManager;
```

This code creates a `TestManager` class which accepts a configuration object with connection details for the external systems and sets up an Express server to handle POST requests at '/runTests'. It then loops through each test function received in the request, executing them and caching their responses. The results of each test are stored in an object and sent back in the response to indicate success or failure of tests with appropriate messages.

This is a simplified version and does not cover all the details you asked for. For example, it doesn't include retries or fallback strategies for external systems failures, nor logging and tracking mechanism. Also, handling concurrent test execution would require proper error handling and synchronization mechanisms, which are beyond the scope of this basic structure. 

