['Here's a high level design of how you could implement this using technologies like Kubernetes for orchestration, Istio as a service mesh, Spring Boot/Spring Cloud for microservices, Keycloak for authentication and authorization, Redis for caching, RabbitMQ for async processing, etc.

```java
// API Gateway Design
@RestController
public class ApiGateway {
    private final LoadBalancerClient loadBalancer;

    @Autowired
    public ApiGateway(LoadBalancerClient loadBalancer) {
        this.loadBalancer = loadBalancer;
    }

    @RequestMapping(value = "/{serviceName}/**", method = RequestMethod.GET)
    public ResponseEntity<?> proxy(@PathVariable("serviceName") String serviceName, HttpServletRequest request) {
        try {
            ServiceInstance instance = loadBalancer.choose(serviceName);

            // Construct the URL for the downstream service and forward the request
            URI downstreamUri = UriComponentsBuilder.fromUriString(instance.getUri().toString())
                    .pathSegment(request.getRequestURI(), new Object[0])
                    .query(request.getQueryString())
                    .build()
                    .toUri();

            // Use RestTemplate to forward the request and get the response
            ResponseEntity<?> response = restTemplate.exchange(downstreamUri, HttpMethod.resolve(request.getMethod()), new HttpEntity<>(null, null), Object.class);

            return response;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Handle exceptions
        }
    }
}
``` 
This design is a high-level abstraction and does not contain the actual implementation of all the features asked for in your problem. It just demonstrates how you could structure an API gateway using Spring Cloud LoadBalancer to dynamically forward requests to downstream services based on the service name provided in the path of the request URL.

