In this example we have two endpoints: `/api/logs` which accepts log entries and append them to a list. The list is protected by a lock so that multiple threads can safely modify it concurrently without causing race conditions. The second endpoint, `/api/external`, applies reconciliation logic based on API responses.

Now let's discuss the C++ function for efficient log processing:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <atomic>
#include <condition_variable>

std::vector<LogEntry> logEntries;  // Assume LogEntry is a struct or class you've defined.
std::mutex mtx;
std::condition_variable cv;
std::atomic<bool> ready(false);

void processLogs(const std::vector<LogEntry>& newLogs) {
    while (!ready) {  // Wait until the processing thread is ready.
        std::this_thread::yield();  // Give up the current time slice to let other threads run.
    }

    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    logEntries.insert(logEntries.end(), newLogs.begin(), newLogs.end());
}

void processingThread() {
    // Perform batch processing and aggregation of log entries here...

    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    ready = true;  // Notify the waiting threads that we're ready.
    cv.notify_all();  // Wake up any threads waiting on the condition variable.
}
```
In this example, `processLogs` is a function which can be called by multiple threads to add new log entries in a thread-safe way. It will wait until the processing thread has started (indicated by `ready`) and then acquire a lock before adding the new logs. After all the logs have been added, it signals that it's ready again with `ready = true` and notifies any waiting threads.

The C++ code is much more complex due to its nature as a compiled language and its lower level of abstraction which means there are many things to consider such as memory management, concurrency control, etc. It's also worth noting that designing the entire system in one response might be too lengthy and complex so I'll leave the deployment instructions and configuration examples for another discussion or a future response.
