```javascript
function topFiveWords(text) {
    const stopWords = ['the', 'and', 'is']; // Add more as needed
    let wordsArray = text.replace(/[.,]/g, "").split(/\s/);

    let wordMap = {};
    for (let i = 0; i < wordsArray.length; i++) {
        if (!stopWords.includes(wordsArray[i].toLowerCase())) { // Ignore stop words
            let currentWord = wordsArray[i];
            if (wordMap[currentWord] === undefined) { 
                wordMap[currentWord] = 1;
            } else {
                wordMap[currentWord]++;
            }
        }
    }

    // Sort the map in descending order and get top 5
    let sortedWords = Object.keys(wordMap).sort((a, b) => wordMap[b] - wordMap[a]);
    return sortedWords.slice(0, 5);
}
```
This function takes a string of text as input and returns an array of the top 5 most frequent words (excluding stop words), ignoring case and punctuation marks. The function works by first removing punctuation and splitting the text into an array of individual words. It then iterates over this array, incrementing a count in a map for each word that isn't a stop word. Finally, it sorts the keys in descending order based on their counts, and returns the top 5 words.
']

