```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <mutex>
#include <vector>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <third_party_dependency.h> // Placeholder for third-party library

class Semaphore {
public:
    Semaphore(int initialCount, int maximumLimit, const std::string& name) : count_(initialCount), maxCount_(maximumLimit), name_(name) {}
    ~Semaphore() = default;

    void acquire() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);
        if (count_ > 0) {
            --count_;
        } else {
            throw std::runtime_error("Semaphore limit reached");
        }
    }

    void release() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);
        if (count_ < maxCount_) {
            ++count_;
        } else {
            throw std::runtime_error("Semaphore limit exceeded");
        }
    }
private:
    std::atomic<int> count_;
    int maxCount_;
    std::string name_;
    std::mutex mtx_;
};
```
This example implements a thread-safe semaphore with basic error handling. The `acquire()` and `release()` methods use RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) to ensure the mutex is always released, regardless of how control leaves the method.

For the distributed tracing framework integration and metrics reporting, you might leverage a library provided by the third-party framework or build such capabilities from scratch using the API provided by the framework. Note that building this functionality from scratch would be quite complex and require good understanding of the underlying implementation details of the system.

