```cpp
#include <random>
#include <mutex>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <iostream>

class MultiThreadedRNG {
private:
    std::mt19937 rng;
    std::recursive_mutex mtx;  // recursive mutex to allow multiple acquisitions from the same thread
public:
    MultiThreadedRNG(uint64_t seed) : rng(seed) { }
    ~MultiThreadedRNG() = default;

    uint32_t getRandomNumber32() {
        std::lock_guard<std::recursive_mutex> lock(mtx);  // automatically releases the mutex when out of scope
        return rng();
    }

    uint64_t getRandomNumber64() {
        std::lock_guard<std::recursive_mutex> lock(mtx);
        uint64_t random = ((uint64_t)rng() << 32) | rng();  // combine two 32-bit outputs into a single 64-bit output
        return random;
    }
};
```
This class encapsulates the `std::mt19937` Mersenne Twister PRNG in its constructor, allowing for thread safety via the use of a recursive mutex. This allows multiple simultaneous calls from the same thread without blocking. 

To integrate this RNG with an existing simulation framework, you would simply instantiate the `MultiThreadedRNG` class and call the appropriate method when you need to generate a random number. The thread-safety of this implementation means that it can be used across multiple threads in your simulation system without causing significant performance degradation.
"]

