['Creating this microservice from scratch would require a lot of code and is beyond the scope of this platform. However, I can certainly give you an idea about how you could structure your project using C# .NET Core with the technologies you mentioned (this would be quite complex to implement fully in one go).

```csharp
using System;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using RestSharp;
using Polly;
using StackExchange.Redis;

[ApiController]
public class WeatherForecastController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly IConnectionMultiplexer _redis;

    public WeatherForecastController(IConnectionMultiplexer redis)
    {
        _redis = redis;
    }

    [HttpGet]
    public async Task<IActionResult> GetWeatherData()
    {
        var client = new RestClient("http://third-party.weather/api");
        var request = new RestRequest(Method.GET);

        // Use Polly for retry logic with exponential backoff
        var policy = Policy
            .Handle<Exception>()
            .WaitAndRetryAsync(3, retryAttempt => TimeSpan.FromSeconds(Math.Pow(2, retryAttempt)));

        // Use Redis for caching data
        var cachedData = await _redis.GetDatabase().StringGetAsync("weather-data");
        if (!cachedData.IsNullOrEmpty)
            return Ok(cachedData);

        // Fetch weather data from third-party API or external database
        WeatherResponse response;
        try
        {
             response = await policy.ExecuteAsync(async () => await client.GetAsync<WeatherResponse>(request));
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            // Fallback if third-party API is not available
            return StatusCode(503, "Third-party weather API is currently unavailable.");
        }

        // Cache the fetched data
        await _redis.GetDatabase().StringSetAsync("weather-data", response);
        return Ok(response);
    }
}
```
The above code uses .NET Core, RestSharp for HTTP requests, Polly for retry logic with exponential backoff, and StackExchange.Redis for distributed caching. It fetches weather data from a third-party API using a retry policy. If the third-party API is not available, it returns a 503 Service Unavailable response. The fetched data is then cached in Redis for future requests to save time and resources.

Please note that this is just an example of how you might start structuring your project. This task involves multiple complex tasks like implementing machine learning models, dealing with system integration complexities, handling regulatory requirements, optimizing performance, creating a CI/CD pipeline etc. These are advanced topics and require in-depth knowledge of the technologies used and proper architectural design.
