```java
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

@RestController
public class TopNIntegersService {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, Integer> topNumbers = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final int N = 50_000;

    @PostMapping("/add")
    public synchronized void addNumber(@RequestParam Integer number) {
        if (topNumbers.size() < N) {
            topNumbers.put(number, 1);
        } else {
            int minCount = Collections.min(topNumbers.values());
            if (number > minCount) {
                topNumbers.remove(Collections.min(topNumbers.entrySet(), Map.Entry.comparingByValue()).getKey());
                topNumbers.put(number, 1);
            }
        }
    }

    @GetMapping("/top")
    public synchronized List<Integer> getTopN() {
        return topNumbers.entrySet().stream()
                .sorted((e1, e2) -> Integer.compare(e2.getValue(), e1.getValue()))
                .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```
This is a very simplified example and doesn't handle network interruptions, packet loss, varying latency, load conditions, dynamic pausing/resuming, data compression, custom data structure (which would be a separate implementation detail), async processing, integration with existing logging frameworks, error handling, resource management, stress testing etc. 

For such complex systems, you should consider using Apache Kafka for streaming data and Spring Cloud Stream for integrating it with Spring Boot microservices. For the fault tolerance part, you may want to use libraries like Resilience4j or Hystrix. Also, check out solutions from platforms like AWS (Kinesis, Lambda) or Google Cloud Pub/Sub.
