['The given task involves designing a complex system spanning multiple layers including memory management, error handling, REST API integration, concurrency, logging, thread safety etc. Below is an example of how this could be implemented in C with the use of glib library for list data structure and libcurl to handle HTTP requests. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <glib.h>
#include <curl/curl.h>

typedef struct URL_NODE {
    char *url;
} URL_NODE;

gboolean uploadFile(char* url, CURL* curl) {
    FILE *file = fopen(url, "r");
     if (!file) {
         return FALSE;
     }

     struct stat fileInfo;
     fstat(fileno(file), &fileInfo);
     long size = fileInfo.st_size;
     char* buffer = malloc(size + 1);
     fread(buffer, size, 1, file);
     fclose(file);

     curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, url);
     curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, buffer);
     curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_VERBOSE, 1L);

     free(buffer);

     return TRUE;
}

gpointer uploadWorker(gpointer data) {
    URL_NODE* node = (URL_NODE*)data;
    CURL *curl = curl_easy_init();
    if(!uploadFile(node->url, curl)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to open file %s\n", node->url);
    } else {
       // Retry policy and backoff strategies can be added here. 
       CURLcode res = curl_easy_perform(curl);
       if (res != CURLE_OK) {
           fprintf(stderr, "Failed to upload file %s:%s\n", node->url, curl_easy_strerror(res));
       } 
    }  

    curl_easy_cleanup(curl);
    free(node->url);
    free(node);
    return NULL;
}

void processURLs(char** urls, int count) {
    GThreadPool* pool = g_thread_pool_new(uploadWorker, count, TRUE, NULL);

    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        URL_NODE* node = malloc(sizeof(URL_NODE));
        node->url = strdup(urls[i]);

        g_thread_pool_push(pool, node, NULL);
    }

    // Clean up thread pool and memory.
    g_thread_pool_free(pool, FALSE, TRUE);
}
```
This is a very basic implementation to illustrate the idea of how one can handle the given problem statement. In real-world scenarios, you would have more checks for error handling, logging mechanism, configurable backoff strategies and retry policy etc., You also need to use appropriate HTTP methods (POST/GET) based on your REST API requirements. Furthermore, managing memory correctly, avoiding leaks or double freeing of memory can be a huge challenge in C due to its manual nature. 

To compile this code, you would typically link against glib and libcurl using gcc as follows: `gcc -o app main.c $(pkg-config --libs --cflags glib-2.0) -l curl`.

Note: This is a highly simplified solution for illustrative purposes only. Real life implementation might be much more complex based on the specifics of your use case, such as thread safety, error handling strategies, network conditions and so on.
