['Implementing this semaphore system in C++ is a complex task that involves multiple layers of integration including distributed tracing, dependency management service, advanced concurrency control mechanism, predictive load balancing, robust logging & error handling, metrics reporting, alert systems, and machine learning model for performance prediction. 

Due to the large scale and complexity of this system, it can't be fully realized within a single code block. However, I will provide an overview of how you might start implementing such a semaphore system with thread-safety, validation and error handling considerations:

```cpp
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <stdexcept>

class Semaphore {
public:
    // Constructor that takes initial count and maximum limit.
    Semaphore(int count = 0, int max = INT_MAX) 
        : mCount(count), mMax(max) {
        if (mCount < 0 || mMax <= 0 || mCount > mMax)
            throw std::logic_error("Invalid semaphore parameters");
    }

    inline void notify() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mMutex);
        if (mCount < mMax) {
            ++mCount;
            mCv.notify_one();
        } else {
            throw std::runtime_error("Semaphore limit exceeded");
        }
    }

    inline void wait() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mMutex);
        while (mCount == 0) {
            mCv.wait(lock);
        }
        --mCount;
    }

private:
    int mMax; // Maximum semaphore limit.
    std::mutex mMutex; 
    std::condition_variable mCv;
    int mCount; // Current resource count.
};
```
Please note that this is a simplified version of a Semaphore implementation and doesn't include all the functionality mentioned in your problem description, such as dynamic adjustment of maximum limits based on runtime conditions or external signals, deletion of semaphores with checks for accidental deletions, advanced logging & error handling, metrics reporting, alert systems, machine learning model etc.

These features require a more complex implementation that involves integration with a distributed tracing and monitoring framework, designing efficient data structures to handle multiple semaphore instances, and implementing concurrency control mechanisms such as mutexes, condition variables, and atomic operations. Furthermore, you'll need to use appropriate design patterns like Singleton or Factory for creating and managing semaphores. 

