```rust
use std::{fs, io};
use envy;

pub fn create_directory_and_set_env(dir_name: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let dir_path = format!["/home/{}", dir_name]; // Assuming a Unix-like directory structure

    match envy::var("MY_DIR") {  // replace MY_DIR with the actual environment variable name
        Ok(dir) if &*dir == dir_path => {
            printlnlnu8;:info: Directory already exists at {}", dir);
        }
        _ => {
            fs::create_dir(&dir_path)?;  // If the directory does not exist, it creates a new one
            envy::set_var("MY_DIR", &*dir_path)?; // Set environment variable to created directory's path
            printlnu8:info: Created new directory at {} and set the MY_DIR environment variable", dir_path);
        }
    };

    Ok(())
}
```
This function first checks if an environment variable named "MY_DIR" is already set to the path of a directory. If it is, it assumes that directory already exists and logs this information. If not or if the environment variable's value does not match our expected directory path, then a new directory is created at `/home/dir_name` (adjust as necessary for your use case), and the "MY_DIR" environment variable is set to point to this new directory.

