["Creating a full-fledged C++ application with all these features in one go is quite complex and beyond the scope of this assistant. However, I can provide you with some code snippets and concepts that could help you get started. 

1) The HTTP response cache: You would need a way to store and retrieve HTTP responses efficiently. A potential solution might be using an LRU (Least Recently Used) cache which automatically removes the least recently used items when the limit is reached. Here's a basic idea of how you can implement such a cache in C++ using an unordered_map for fast lookup and a doubly-linked list for maintaining LRU order:

```cpp
#include <unordered_map>
#include <list>
#include <string>

class HttpResponse {
    // class definition...
};

class LruCache {
private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::pair<HttpResponse, std::list<std::string>::iterator>> cache;
    std::list<std::string> lruList;

public:
    HttpResponse get(const std::string& key) {
        auto it = cache.find(key);

        if (it == cache.end()) {
            // handle cache miss...
        } else {
            lruList.erase(it->second.second);
            lruList.push_front(key);
            return it->second.first;
        }
    }

    void put(const std::string& key, const HttpResponse& response) {
        auto it = cache.find(key);

        if (it != cache.end()) {
            lruList.erase(it->second.second);
        } else {
            // handle maximum limit...
        }

        lruList.push_front(key);
        cache[key] = std::make_pair(response, lruList.begin());
    }

    // more methods for handling eviction, clearing the cache, etc...
};
```

2) Concurrent access: You would need to ensure that the cache is thread-safe. One way to do this in C++ is by using a std::mutex to protect access to the unordered_map and doubly-linked list. However, this can limit performance due to contention for the lock. If high concurrency is common, you may need to consider other synchronization primitives such as std::shared_mutex or fine-grained locks.

